The generated table can hold recipe IDs rather than duplicate emitter code. For $k$ Boolean signature tests a flat array has $2^k$ slots, so an opcode with many sparse tests may be better served by ordered rows or a small decision tree. To test a generator, compare its table entries with an independent linear scan of the ordered rules; to test the rules themselves, compare the emitted behavior to the IR. An unsupported opcode or an impossible signature is a compiler error, not permission to silently omit the operation.

Blindell notes that naive expanders were limited principally by visiting one node at a time, not by an inability to dispatch faster (\cite[chapter~2, sections~2.2.3--2.2.4]{blindell2016instruction}). The table in this example cannot tell whether the producer of a register argument and the store could merge, because the producer is absent from its key. More context could be added, but ad hoc state shared between macros can invite backtracking and make coverage harder to audit. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-264} shows another design: emit a legal baseline, then combine compatible effects across a bounded window.
